During our protest to end horseracing in Bangalore, activists spoke about a fundamental mindset of people when they think of horses, Horses are meant for riding. {.justify}

“How many of you have ridden horses, or sat on a horse before? 🐴” {.justify}

Sai Lohith questions our right to use their body. {.justify}

“We call it a “partnership” but horses never signed up. {.justify}

Horseriding = breaking their spirits, metal bits in their mouth, whips and damaged spines for our entertainment. {.justify}

They deserve to run free, not carry us. Let horses be horses.” {.justify}

Saadhana reflected on her childhood experience of riding a pony named Majnu, the regret she feels now, and the urge to end this slavery. {.justify}

We have heard a million times from the horse people that there is a right way to ride, train, breed, and stable horses. It's just horseracing that is evil, or horseracing and equine sports can be reformed, or horses are good for human therapy and exercise. It is really hard to keep up with the pseudoscience and pseudophilosophy in the horse world, because it takes less effort to spew up horseshit than to debunk it. {.justify}

There is scientific evidence to show that sitting on horses’ backs causes them pain, mouth bits cause them pain, whipping causes them pain, and tongue ties cause pain. Somehow the only thing the horse people look for is how to ride and control horses by causing them less pain and no question of whether they ever should be riding or controlling them. {.justify}

The philosophical reasoning is sound, as horses are sentient beings worth our moral consideration. They possess the right to live free from exploitation and murder. Imagine if someone were finding ways to painlessly rape a human for their pleasure or for a living. Whose side will you take? The victims’ or the oppressors’? {.justify}

Horses want to be free as much as humans do. Let’s speak up for them as we would for humans. {.justify}

the_rightmind_ | Sai Pooja M G spoke at the Jana Pragne interview about an injustice we must question. {.justify}

“Behind every ride is a horse who didn’t get a choice. Cruelty isn’t entertainment 💔 {.justify}

ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದು ಸವಾರಿಯ ಹಿಂದೆಯೂ, ತನಗೆ ಆಯ್ಕೆಯ ಅವಕಾಶವೇ ಇಲ್ಲದ ಒಂದು ಕುದುರೆ ಇರುತ್ತದೆ. ಕ್ರೌರ್ಯವು ಮನರಂಜನೆಯಲ್ಲ 💔” {.justify}

The bit, often defended as a tool of communication, is in reality a cruel instrument of control. They force a horse to wear a bit. Resisting it is made more painful, so the horse eventually gives in. A 2020 study explains how bits cause intense pain. Try the “Mellor pen test” to feel it for yourself. More details below. {.justify}

The “Mellor pen-test” is intended to simulate the bit pressure applied to the gums of the horse. {.justify}

- Hold the pen in front of your mouth.
- Open your mouth.
- Roll your bottom lip down and locate the pen on your gum, below your central incisors.
- Now release your lip and with both hands holding the pen, apply compressive pressure to your gum.

How much pain can you withstand? {.justify}

Now, lying on your back, imagine a bag hanging from the ends of the pen with a 7 kg weight, the maximum for carry-on, and then a 20 kg weight, the maximum for stowed luggage. {.justify}

This is, however, less than half the maximum force that a horse experiences from the rein in tension. {.justify}

The [2020 study](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32235343/) by David J. Mellor in Animals explained how horses show a strong aversion to bits, which can cause intense breathlessness, panic, and anxiety. The bit’s placement also interferes with a horse’s ability to breathe freely, producing sensations of suffocation that drive fear and distress. {.justify}

Far from the illusion of a “cooperative” racehorse prancing eagerly at the gate, the behaviors we see, wide eyes, gaping mouths, teeth grinding, salivation, tongue lolling, head shaking, and tail swishing, are [not signs of excitement but of agony](https://horseracingwrongs.org/2022/04/27/cpr-australia-the-extraordinary-cruelty-of-the-bit/). These are horses desperately reacting to pain they cannot escape. When the industry calls this “communication,” it conceals a harsh reality: horses are not willing participants but terrified, helpless victims forced to obey through pain. {.justify}

Don’t watch. Don’t bet. Don’t attend.

Boycott horseracing

Link to the article: [https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/4/572](https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/4/572) {.justify}

Sources: CPR and TCG Studioz {.justify}

The racing industry wants you to believe that the whip is a “tool.”
But science is clear: horses feel the sting of every strike just like we would. {.justify}

**Horse skin = Human skin** {.justify}

Professor Paul McGreevy’s team found: {.justify}

- No difference in skin thickness between horses & humans
- Same concentration of nerve endings
- Same sensitivity to pain

📖 Source: McGreevy et al., Animals (2020a) {.justify}

**Horses feel whip pain just like humans.** {.justify}

“This was not surprising, as horses, like humans, need robust yet sensitive skin to respond to touch, say, from flying insects or other horses,” said Professor McGreevy. {.justify}

If we’d feel the sting of a whip, so do they. {.justify}

📖 Source: University of Sydney News (November 2020) {.justify}

**What do other experts say?** {.justify}

- Whips don’t make horses run faster
- Whips don’t improve safety
- Whips only cause fear and pain

📖 Sources: {.justify}

Racing Pulse (September 2025) {.justify}

Hall et al., J Appl Anim Welf Sci (2008) {.justify}

McGreevy et al., Animals (2020b) {.justify}

Whip bans won’t fix racing.
Horses are still exploited, broken, and killed for profit. {.justify}

Don’t watch. Don’t attend. Don’t bet.

Boycott horseracing.
